			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852874">
									

<p>Summoned by PM</p>
<p>The premise here is an interesting one depending on what you do with it. It would be very easy to turn this into a &quot;thing that does a thing&quot; with nothing else - essentially that's what the anomaly is - but if you do something interesting narratively and really explore the effects of this anomaly properly, I think this could be an interesting premise for an article.</p>
<p>Exploring a series of unique vignettes could work, but you'd need to find a way to build them towards a conclusive end that leaves the reader feeling satisfied, and that won't be super easy to do. I'd recommend trying to build a more traditional narrative for your first article. Give us a clear series of events that you're going to tell - that's the core of any good SCP article, a clear narrative. If you've got that, you're on a strong start. The anomaly doesn't necessarily lend itself to a traditional narrative without a bit of work, but you've started that process by having it create an effect in people who watch their own perspective. Try to expand on that in terms of what stories you might tell with it, perhaps. Maybe it causes some serious problems that the Foundation then has to solve, or perhaps it's an addictive feeling, or maybe the Foundation thinks they can use the SCP to try and control people and anomalies in some way.</p>
<p>Whatever you go with, the core thing this needs is a clearer central narrative, with a beginning, middle, and end. What's the story you're going to tell here, in detail? That's the key.</p>

							</div>

								
					
