														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852845">
									

<p>Quoting Silence of the Lambs as a &quot;socially responsible&quot; movie is especially concerning, considering it was widely panned by LGBT audiences for its incredibly irresponsible depiction of transsexuality (no matter how much it tried to backtrack with its Blanchardist &quot;fake trans&quot; pseudoscientific nonsense).</p>
<p>An important thing to keep in mind when you're handling sensitive topics: what are you actually trying to say? A charitable take on the piece for me is that it's trying to make a point about how misogyny is ingrained into the Japanese traditionalist zeitgeist; however, it doesn't feel like there's any attempt to analyze <em>how</em> that's the case. Instead, it feels like a titillating backdrop to an action-horror piece, or at least that's what I got from the Central Narrative.</p>
<p>This is vitally important for any piece handling sensitive topics, because the alternative is letting your piece degenerate into an &quot;exploitation piece&quot;, e.g. something that depicts the topics with an almost voyeuristic aloofness and no real attempt to make a point on it, which severely limits the audience your piece can engage with.</p>

							</div>

